{
  "heading": "Listener options: condition, Order and InBackground",
  "axis": "language",
  "body": "A listener hears one model, the type of its parameter. It can also have a condition. A condition is a small, limited expression that uses value and event. Tachyon compiles it once into a matcher, and every runtime evaluates that matcher. Order(n) runs the listeners of one event from the lowest number up. Listeners with the same number run in the order you declare them. A listener without Order has the number 0. A listener publishes again when its return type is a model: the value it returns, or each of up to 32 values in a returned list, is published as that model. Tachyon ignores any other return value. InBackground makes a listener run later, after the current delivery pass. Such a detached listener never publishes again, as in Spring. In Tac, InBackground works in every language, because the page's event bus delays the delivery itself. In Yon, JavaScript, TypeScript and Python accept it, and so do Lua, Java and Groovy, whose event runtimes run the listener after the other listeners of the same event. The other Yon languages reject it at compile time: PHP, Kotlin, C#, F#, Rust, Dart, Go and Ruby. An id names a listener in diagnostics (error and warning messages).",
